Code statements:
```c
if (timeout - READ_ONCE(flow->timeout) > HZ)
    WRITE_ONCE(flow->timeout, timeout);
else
    return;
```

The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
timeout = nf_flowtable_time_stamp + flow_offload_get_timeout(flow);
if (READ_ONCE(flow->timeout) != timeout)
```
Reason:
The code checks if the current timeout has changed and attempts to update it without any delay or limit on how often this check occurs. This results in continuous retries to offload the flow immediately after a failure, leading to excessive system load.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (timeout - READ_ONCE(flow->timeout) > HZ)
    WRITE_ONCE(flow->timeout, timeout);
else
    return;
```
Reason:
The fix introduces a condition that checks if the time since the last timeout update exceeds one second (defined by `HZ`). This effectively throttles the retry attempts, allowing only one update per second, which reduces the system load caused by excessive offload attempts.

